 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Rosita Missoni knew how to travel: comfortably, lightly, and with just enough cultivated eccentricity to make practicality look instinctive. Alberto Caliri, who worked alongside her, took that spirit as his starting point for a collection about movement rather than destination. The show itself had a pleasing beat to it, unfolding in the cloisters of San Simpliciano, where a beautiful garden and fountains provided an appropriately bucolic backdrop. The clothes suggested a woman who dresses on the go, adding and subtracting according to the moment: a crumple-brimmed hat for shelter, thick socks tucked into delicate sandals, a soft bag thrown over a voluminous dress. Nothing looked quite planned, which was precisely the point.</p><p>The idea of the nomad, Caliri said, was less about geography than about movement and possibility. Long, fluid shapes, balloon trousers, low-slung pants, easy peignoirs, chenille pieces and tabards gathered with drawstrings created a flow with plenty of volume but remarkably little fuss. Chevrons, stripes and familiar Missoni geometries loosened, frayed and almost dissolved, threaded with sequins and metallic glints. Rough and luscious, matte and iridescent, “they had something of the outside and inside of a shell,” Caliri noted.</p><p>The styling had the same freedom: “it’s about getting dressed without overthinking it,” said the designer. Day and evening, useful and decorative, humble and precious were mixed up. Missoni has always known that clothes can travel; this season, they looked as though they had simply decided to go wherever they pleased.</p></div>",
